#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"}] :question/id #uuid "9bdb609d-4000-5ee3-9267-71ed6a8dd140" :question/math-academy-id "e-7392" :question/problem "It is known that $75\\%$ of students pass a particular exam. If $5$ students who took the exam are randomly selected, what is the probability that $3$ of them passed the exam? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of students that passed the exam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ students\n- the probability that each student passed the exam is $p=75\\%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(5,0.75)$. We want to find the probability that $x=3$ of the students passed the exam.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 3) &= (\\frac{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1 - 0.75)^{5 - 3} \\\\\n&= (5!)/(3!(5 - 3)!)(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\\n&= 10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.264\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105584 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable Over an Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "cbc5bcb5-2c64-5895-ba1c-cc6d86207667" :question/math-academy-id "e-7393" :question/problem "A standard fair die is rolled $8$ times. What is the probability of rolling an even number at least $7$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ rolls\n- the probability of rolling an even number on any roll of the die is\n$p=\\dfrac{3}{6} = 0.5$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(8,0.5)$.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = x) &= (\\frac{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1 - 0.5)^{8 - x} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{8 - x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of rolling an even number at least $7$ times. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 7) &= P(X = 7) + P(X = 8) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{8 - 7} + (\\frac{8}{8})(0.5)^{8}(0.5)^{8 - 8} \\\\\n&= 8(0.5)^{7}(0.5) + 1(0.5)^{8} \\\\\n&= 0.035\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105585 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "1fab59f6-f640-5533-9bff-4946dfd71f77" :question/math-academy-id "e-7394" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n1. The ages of $20$ college professors\n2. The number of questions answered correctly by a student in a $12$-question exam\n3. The heights of $10$ elementary school children" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of questions answered in a $12$- question exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $12$ independent experiments.\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The heights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105588 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Distribution Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "b4cfa71b-69f8-5cdb-b3c9-6dabdb32e264" :question/math-academy-id "e-7444" :question/problem "In a random card game, there is a $40\\%$ chance of winning a game. If the player plays $12$ games, what is the probability that he wins at least $2$ of the games? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the player wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ games\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=40\\%=0.4$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(12,0.4)$.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = x) &= (\\frac{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1 - 0.4)^{12 - x} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{12 - x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player wins at least 2 of the games. This corresponds to\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2)\n$$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2) = P(X=2) + P(X=3) + \\ldots + P(X=12)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 2) &= 1 - P(X < 2) \\\\\n&= 1 - [P(X = 0) + P(X = 1)] \\\\\n&= 1 - [(\\frac{12}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{12 - 0} + (\\frac{12}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{12 - 1}] \\\\\n&= 1 - [(0.6)^{12} + 12(0.4)(0.6)^{11}] \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.019591 \\\\\n&= 0.980\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81196c88-2f39-5d59-b054-6de027c253ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105150} {:answer/id #uuid "ad082999-6b55-531f-bc26-350c9f5278ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105151} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a4babb-936e-5ab7-a519-4c03fded1379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105152} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7fd31c-0837-5cf3-8521-784dec76bf30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105153} {:answer/id #uuid "196f4575-dcd6-56ce-b34d-211a58321a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "196f4575-dcd6-56ce-b34d-211a58321a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105154} :answer-field/id #uuid "a36d4fad-fb40-517e-b0bc-6e0e57f916c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105149}] :question/id #uuid "af8a038a-87f9-56f5-bd05-c7deeebc5a7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-84043" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n1. The weights of $30$ professional wrestlers\n2. The number of times that the number $6$ appears when a fair die is rolled $20$ times\n3. The price of milk at the store"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Distribution Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af0a1526-6bb6-547e-b2af-9645eb0b6551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.883" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105180} {:answer/id #uuid "ccef99fc-d357-58cd-a2b1-e74cc3162faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.674" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105181} {:answer/id #uuid "d747413a-17ff-5123-92bf-51b0e853c423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.608" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105182} {:answer/id #uuid "11eb39a4-2c3e-52fe-9eb2-6d703a5dd28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105183} {:answer/id #uuid "1c3966ae-3ab9-5161-a5cb-55343a2a671b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.621" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d747413a-17ff-5123-92bf-51b0e853c423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.608" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105182} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d2e4076-ef1e-5bf9-af18-c31317fa2d3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105179}] :question/id #uuid "89d84ad1-9c54-50de-b286-c51160255480" :question/math-academy-id "q-84153" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA golfer has a $10%$ chance of winning each tournament they enter. If the golfer enters $20$ tournaments, what is the probability that they win at least $2$ tournaments? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
